1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:06,544
Model Context Protocol, or M C P, is a way
for a client to connect to servers that

2
00:00:06,544 --> 00:00:09,193
can supply tools and other context.

3
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:14,536
Our simulated server exposes one tool that
reads a synthetic training issue.

4
00:00:14,716 --> 00:00:19,591
We will follow the request from the
approved GCP client, through the server,

5
00:00:19,591 --> 00:00:23,311
and back to the session where the result
becomes evidence.

6
00:00:24,757 --> 00:00:28,811
Begin with the workspace's approval record
for the training server.

7
00:00:28,991 --> 00:00:32,520
Confirm its source, environment, and
available tools.

8
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:38,301
The operation here reads a single issue by
number, in a synthetic project only.

9
00:00:38,481 --> 00:00:43,185
Confirm the actual access scope with the
owner or supplied lab policy.

10
00:00:43,365 --> 00:00:47,774
A friendly tool name only helps you
identify the action, so check the

11
00:00:47,774 --> 00:00:49,691
effective permissions as well.

12
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:55,590
Open the approved integration settings in
Claude Code or OpenCode.

13
00:00:55,770 --> 00:01:01,255
Configuration fields and locations vary by
client and version, so read what the

14
00:01:01,255 --> 00:01:02,574
workspace provides.

15
00:01:02,754 --> 00:01:05,285
Look for the configured identity and
scope.

16
00:01:05,465 --> 00:01:10,930
If the integration is unavailable in the
training workspace, follow along with the

17
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:15,130
same input and recorded result supplied as
a synthetic fixture.

18
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:20,920
Ask the agent for the acceptance criteria
of issue seventeen.

19
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:25,079
Before the call runs, inspect the proposed
tool and its argument.

20
00:01:25,259 --> 00:01:27,823
Make sure the request points to the
training issue.

21
00:01:28,003 --> 00:01:31,056
Follow the session's permission prompt if
one appears.

22
00:01:31,236 --> 00:01:36,117
This is the point where you verify that
the planned call matches both your intent

23
00:01:36,117 --> 00:01:37,805
and the approved data scope.

24
00:01:39,257 --> 00:01:42,161
Read the result returned from the training
server.

25
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:45,896
The issue provides two straightforward
acceptance criteria.

26
00:01:46,076 --> 00:01:49,820
Check each item in the agent's summary
against the response.

27
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,629
The tool call supplies context for the
model.

28
00:01:52,809 --> 00:01:57,707
Its text can describe the issue, but
consequential actions still follow your

29
00:01:57,707 --> 00:02:00,221
instruction and the policy of the host.

30
00:02:01,673 --> 00:02:06,362
Look at the training permission record and
check what happens when a proposed follow-

31
00:02:06,362 --> 00:02:08,458
up would write back to the repository.

32
00:02:08,638 --> 00:02:12,487
The scope only covers reads, so keep the
summary in the session.

33
00:02:12,667 --> 00:02:18,405
In the engineering workflow, a person
reviews the pull request using GitHub CLI.

34
00:02:18,585 --> 00:02:23,205
This keeps the source of evidence and the
authorization to act easy to audit.

35
00:02:24,673 --> 00:02:29,313
Record the server name, the tool, the
issue number, and what you verified from

36
00:02:29,313 --> 00:02:30,086
the response.

37
00:02:30,266 --> 00:02:34,831
Add the client version if discovery or
approvals were central to this lab.

38
00:02:35,011 --> 00:02:38,979
You now have a small repeatable
integration with a clear boundary.

39
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:44,055
The next episode adds checks that can stop
a workflow when its output misses an

40
00:02:44,055 --> 00:02:45,357
explicit requirement.
